    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="17" relname="span">Error recovery is an important attribute to consider .</segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="19" relname="span">Preece,Sharp and Rogers said "Interaction Design" that we evaluate an interface to answers these questions:</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="2" relname="elaboration">-Are error messages helpful?</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="19" relname="elaboration">-Do they use plain language to describe the nature of the problem and suggest away of solving it?</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="20" relname="span">Errors are bound to occur</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="5" relname="volitional-result">and users aren'talways experts with some interface and need to be able to recover from errors as painlessly as possible.</segment>
        <segment id="7" parent="15" relname="span">Also,efficiency refers to the way a system supports users in carrying out their tasks.</segment>
        <segment id="8" parent="7" relname="elaboration">We have to always keep in mind that in auser centered design approach the taskd a user will carry out must be understood.</segment>
        <segment id="9" parent="15" relname="elaboration">We have to learn what taskd users will be trying to accomplish with the system.</segment>
        <segment id="10" parent="11" relname="circumstance">Once we understand the requirements set by the users tasks</segment>
        <segment id="11" parent="16" relname="span">then we can make the interface efficient to accomplish these tasks.</segment>
        <group id="12" type="span"/>
        <group id="13" type="span" parent="12" relname="span"/>
        <group id="14" type="span" parent="21" relname="span"/>
        <group id="15" type="span" parent="14" relname="span"/>
